A computer connected locally is on a LAN (3). A local area network covers devices in the same place, like a home or an office.
Linking offices in two buildings a few streets apart needs a WAN (2). A LAN only covers one site, and a WAN connects LANs together.
Browsing google.com on mobile data (not Wi-Fi) means you're on the Internet (1). Your phone goes through the carrier's network and out to the public Internet.